\t})\n})\n```\n\nWait — there's a subtlety. Since these tests use `Expect` inside the handler (goroutine on the server side), Gomega assertions in goroutines can fail. But actually the standard pattern from the controller's test does exactly this — handler asserts with Gomega's `Expect`. In Ginkgo v2, using `Expect` in goroutines is supported but failures in goroutines might not propagate correctly. Actually, Ginkgo v2 has `gomega` with `DeferCleanup` etc. Actually the safest way is to record the request values and assert after the call returns. But the prompt says \"mirror the assertion style of the controller's `git_rest_client_test.go`: the handler asserts `r.Method`, `r.URL.Path`, and `r.URL.Query()` on each request\".\n\nLet me check the controller's test to see exactly. The controller module isn't in the cache. Let me try to download it. Actually the prompt says \"If that module is not present in the container's module cache, the target code below is authoritative\". Let me try downloading it to see the test style.\n\nActually, using `Expect` inside the httptest handler goroutine with Ginkgo — Ginkgo v2 supports assertions from goroutines as long as the suite is not in parallel mode; it will fail the spec. Actually, this is a common pattern in many repos. Let me just mirror it but be safe: I could capture values into variables and assert in the main goroutine. But to \"mirror the controller's test style\" I should check the actual controller test.\n\nLet me download the agent-task-controller module.","signature":""}],"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ab2e800e-9400-4e49-90cb-78b86646bc3f","uuid":"1843ff0f-de35-4126-8509-2f6a83869ca8","timestamp":"2026-09-03T18:20:52.110Z"}
